<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/expert/jurisprudence-what-is-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Jurisprudence asks the question every other law lesson assumes away: <strong>what makes a
    rule "law" at all?</strong> The answers matter practically — they decide whether courts must
    enforce evil statutes, whether judges discover law or make it, and how much discretion the
    system tolerates. Four schools have carried the argument for two centuries.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four schools — and the Nazi-grudge case that forced them into the open" widget="equationStep" config={jurisCfg} />

  <h2>Why the theory is practical</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interpretation is theory in disguise:</strong> textualists, intentionalists and purposivists are carrying jurisprudential positions into every judgment — the "theory vs practice" divide is fiction.</li>
    <li><strong>Dworkin's intervention:</strong> law includes PRINCIPLES, not just rules — judges best-fit the law as a moral story ("Hercules" judge). The third way between Hart and Fuller, still argued.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical schools:</strong> legal realism radicalised — Critical Legal Studies (law serves power), feminist jurisprudence (MacKinnon), critical race theory (Bell, Crenshaw). Each asks: whose interests does the "neutral" system actually serve?</li>
    <li><strong>Why a lawyer needs this:</strong> every hard case is hard because rules run out — and what fills the gap is the theorist's question, answered live.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hart, The Concept of Law (1961); Fuller, "Positivism and Fidelity to Law" (1958); Dworkin, Law's Empire (1986); Holmes, "The Path of the Law" (1897).</p>
</TopicPage>
